    "product_id": "d5705f70-d221-4841-a624-cb7805b960ab",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 110541\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1041 PM MST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\n.UPDATE...06Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- A Flood Warning is in effect through at least early next week\n  along the Gila River between Painted Rock Dam and Dateland,\n  Arizona due to water released from the dam. \n\n- Dry and tranquil weather with slightly above normal temperatures\n  will continue through middle of the week.\n\n- A more unsettled pattern is expected by the weekend through\n  early next week with increasing rain chances and cooler\n  temperatures as a series of weather systems move through the\n  Desert Southwest.\n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nLatest objective analysis shows upper-level ridging prevailing\nover the Desert Southwest. This ridging pattern will remain in \nplace through the middle of the week before the pattern shifts by\nthe end of the week. As a result, expect a continuation of dry and\ntranquil weather conditions along with mostly clear skies.\nTemperatures through Thursday will solidly be around 5 degrees \nabove normal as afternoon highs across much of the lower desert \ncommunities top out in the mid 80s. Overnight and early morning \nlows will also be mild with readings in the mid 50s to around 60 \ndegrees. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nA clear pattern shift later this week is likely to lead to a\nquasi-blocking flow regime as the flow becoming highly amplified\nover the eastern Pacific through the entire CONUS and Canada. The\nridge over the Intermountain West is expected to extend into \nsouthern Canada by Thursday with deep troughing developing off the\nWest Coast and over the Northeastern U.S. Despite the highly \namplified flow, the upstream trough to our northwest should be \nenough to kick the ridge just to the east of our region later \nThursday into Friday as a modest shortwave trough peels off from \nthe parent large scale trough. Model guidance is still struggling \nto come to a full consensus on the track and the strength of the \nincoming disturbance for Friday into Saturday. Trends over the \npast couple of days have shifted the system more over Baja and \nSonora Mexico while also showing some noticeable weakening as it \ntracks by just to our south on Friday. A second shortwave trough \nmay also track by just to our north on Saturday.\n\nDespite the lingering model uncertainty later this week, we are\nnearly assured to have increasing higher level clouds early on \nFriday followed by some weak lower level moisture advection out of\nthe south during the latter half of Friday. Ensemble mean PWATs \nare shown to increase to between 175-200% of normal starting \nFriday, but due to the bulk of the system energy likely bypassing \nto the south on Friday and to the north on Saturday we are not \nanticipating widespread rainfall. NBM PoPs are still mostly \nbetween 10-25% for the Arizona lower deserts to 20-35% for the \nhigher terrain north and east of the Phoenix area from midday \nFriday through much of Saturday. Rainfall is expected to be light\nwith total amounts of a trace to at most 0.10\" across the lower \ndeserts to localized amounts of 0.10-0.20\" over the high terrain. \nWe also can't completely rule out a weak thunderstorm or two on \nFriday focused across far southern Arizona since forecast MUCAPE \ndoes increase to 200-300 J/kg. Temperatures will start to drop \non Friday with lower desert highs falling back into the upper 70s\nto around 80 degrees followed by mid to upper 70s over the \nweekend. The unsettled weather pattern is expected to continue \ninto next week with even better rain chances by around next \nTuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0540Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo aviation weather concerns are expected through the forecast\nperiod under mostly clear skies. Winds will remain light, mainly\nAOB 7 kts, and maintain an easterly component through tonight and\nmuch of the day tomorrow. KIWA may see a NW shift late in the \nafternoon, however there is low confidence that a westerly shift \nwill occur at KPHX.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo aviation concerns are anticipated at the southeast CA terminals\nthrough the TAF period under clear skies. Winds will favor a NW-W\ncomponent at KIPL and N-NE component at KBLH. Extended periods of\nlight (below 5 kts) and VRB winds to nearly calm conditions are \nanticipated at both terminals.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions and slightly above normal temperatures will \nprevail through at least Thursday. Minimum humidity values will \nhover near 25-40% across eastern districts and 20-30% in western \ndistricts. This follows fair to good overnight recovery near \n30-60% in western areas and good to excellent levels 50-80% in \neastern areas. Expect overall light winds, but also some \nmarginally breezy conditions across ridge tops and terrain gaps \nof eastern districts during the morning hours. A more unsettled\nweather pattern is expected to settle over the region by the\nweekend increasing moisture and rain chances and dropping\ntemperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...\n\nLower Salt: Water releases above 1000 cfs continue from the \nBartlett to Granite Reef Dams. Therefore, a Flood Warning remains\nin effect through Wednesday morning between Granite Reef Dam and \nTempe Town Lake with low-water crossings impacted. \n\nGila: A Flood Warning is in effect from Painted Rock Dam through \nthe Dateland area through at least early next week due to water \nreleased from the dam. Water release levels are expected to \nincrease during the next couple of days, peaking at 3000 cfs. \nThese increased releases will have the potential to flood farm \nfields and low-water crossings along the river downstream from \nthe dam.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman \nAVIATION...Salerno \nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman \nHYDROLOGY...Lojero\n",
